Record Types {#chapter-types}
=======================================

## Definition ## {#section-types-definition}

Every [=Record=] MUST be assigned to a <dfn>Record Type</dfn>. A Record type:

- defines the schema of the [=Payload=]
- defines the permitted states and their transitions
- defines the format requirements per state
- defines mandatory and optional [=Metadata=] fields (see [Metadata](#chapter-metadata))
- is itself a [=Record=] of the type `SchemaRecord`


## Core Record Types ## {#section-record-types-core}

RWP defines the following core [=Record types=]:

**Information Records:**
- `ObjectRecord` (TBD)
- `DocumentRecord` (TBD)

**Relation Records:**
- `CaseRecord` (see [CaseRecord Specification](#chapter-caserecord))
- `AssertionRecord` (see [AssertionRecord  Specification](#chapter-assertionrecord))

**Agent Records:**
- Various (see [AgentRecord Specification](#chapter-agentrecord))

**System Records:**
- `SchemaRecord` (see [SchemaRecord](#chapter-schema-record))
- `ConformanceRecord` (see [Conformance and Implementation Claims](#chapter-conformance))
- `DeletionRecord` (see [Payload Deletion](#chapter-deletion))
- `MergeRecord` (TBD)
- `MigrationRecord` (TBD)

The conformance requirements for these [=Record types=] are profile-, role-, and capability-specific. An implementation MUST support a core [=Record type=] only where this specification requires that support for a claimed profile, role, or exercised capability.

The capability-bound requirements are defined in [Capability-bound requirements](#section-conformance-requirements).

Additional [=Record types=] MAY be defined by organisations or standards bodies. They MUST be published as [=SchemaRecords=] and be resolvable via a DID.


## Taxonomy ## {#section-types-taxonomy}

```text
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix rw:   <https://w3id.org/recordweb/ontology#> .

rw:Record
  rdfs:subClassOf prov:Entity .

rw:InformationRecord
  rdfs:subClassOf rw:Record .

rw:RelationRecord
  rdfs:subClassOf rw:Record .

rw:SystemRecord
  rdfs:subClassOf rw:Record .

rw:ObjectRecord
  rdfs:subClassOf rw:InformationRecord .

rw:DocumentRecord
  rdfs:subClassOf rw:InformationRecord .

rw:CaseRecord
  rdfs:subClassOf rw:RelationRecord .

rw:AssertionRecord
  rdfs:subClassOf rw:RelationRecord .

rw:Agent
  rdfs:subClassOf prov:Agent .

rw:AgentRecord
  rdfs:subClassOf rw:Agent .

rw:SchemaRecord
  rdfs:subClassOf rw:SystemRecord .

rw:MergeRecord
  rdfs:subClassOf rw:SystemRecord .

rw:DeletionRecord
  rdfs:subClassOf rw:SystemRecord .

rw:MigrationRecord
  rdfs:subClassOf rw:SystemRecord .

rw:ConformanceRecord
  rdfs:subClassOf rw:SystemRecord .

```

Every [=Record=] has one primary conceptual purpose. For the purposes of this specification, a [=Record=] is classified as an [=InformationRecord=], a [=RelationRecord=], an [=AgentRecord=] or a [=SystemRecord=].

Any concrete Record Type is defined by a SchemaRecord in accordance with [Record Type Definition](#section-record-types-definition).

The taxonomy is normative for the interpretation of this specification. It MUST NOT be interpreted as a mandatory implementation inheritance hierarchy, a mandatory JSON type hierarchy, or a replacement for concrete [=Record Types=] and their SchemaRecords.

An <dfn>InformationRecord</dfn> is a Record whose primary purpose is to carry semantically autonomous institutional or domain information.

An <dfn>ObjectRecord</dfn> is an [=InformationRecord=]. TBD

A <dfn>DocumentRecord</dfn> is an [=InformationRecord=]. TBD

A <dfn>RelationRecord</dfn> is a Record whose primary purpose is to establish, preserve, and make provable a meaningful relationship between Records.

A <dfn>CaseRecord</dfn> is a [=RelationRecord=]. It represents a persisted, governed view of linked Records. A CaseRecord MUST NOT be a container and MUST NOT contain the payload of the Records to which it relates. The protocol representation and validation requirements for CaseRecords are defined in [CaseRecord Specification](#chapter-caserecord).

An <dfn>AssertionRecord</dfn> is a [=RelationRecord=]. It represents an attributable assertion that a subject resource stands in a specified relationship to an object resource. The protocol representation and validation requirements for AssertionRecords are defined in [AssertionRecord Specification](#chapter-assertionrecord).

An <dfn>AgentRecord</dfn> is a Record whose primary purpose is to identifies an entity that bears responsibility for a [=Record=], in the sense of prov:Agent ([[PROVO]]).

A <dfn>SystemRecord</dfn> is a Record whose primary purpose is to define, document, or prove a RecordWeb system rule, operation, or capability.

A <dfn>SchemaRecord</dfn> is a [=SystemRecord=]. It defines a concrete Record Type and its associated schema and validation requirements defined in [SchemaRecord Specification](#chapter-schemarecord).

A <dfn>MergeRecord</dfn> is a [=SystemRecord=]. TBD

A <dfn>DeletionRecord</dfn> is a [=SystemRecord=]. See [DeletionRecord Specification](#chapter-deletionrecord).

A <dfn>MigrationRecord</dfn> is a [=SystemRecord=]. TBD

A <dfn>ConformanceRecord</dfn> is a [=SystemRecord=]. TBD


